Assume such a map exists and fix a tetrahedron $S$ of its support complex. Let $t$ be the number of blocks in Lemma~ef{lem:partner-blocks}, and let $R$ be the set of labels outside their union. The blocks contain $4+t$ labels in total, so \[ 1\le t\le4,\qquad |R|=6-t. \] If $t=4$, each block has size two and $S$ meets each in just one element, contradicting the last assertion of Lemma~ef{lem:partner-blocks}. Thus $t\le3$. There is no triangle entirely in $R$. By purity, such a triangle would extend to a tetrahedron; its one remaining vertex could not supply two vertices in a block. On the other hand, $R$ is contained in $[10]\setminus S$, where every four labels contain a triangle by Lemmas~ef{lem:facet-complement} and~ef{lem:six-properties}(2). Consequently $|R|\le3$, and therefore $t\ge3$. It follows that $t=3$, $|R|=3$, and the block sizes are $3,2,2$, as in Figure~ef{fig:partner-blocks}.  egin{figure}[tbp] \centering  egin{tikzpicture}[ x=1cm,y=1cm,font=\small, present/.style={circle,fill=black,inner sep=1.7pt}, omitted/.style={circle,draw=black,fill=white,inner sep=1.6pt,line width=.7pt}, every path/.style={line width=.6pt} ] \draw[rounded corners=3pt] (0,.35) rectangle (3,1.8); \draw[rounded corners=3pt] (3.55,.35) rectangle (5.55,1.8); \draw[rounded corners=3pt] (6.1,.35) rectangle (8.1,1.8); \draw[rounded corners=3pt,densely dashed] (8.8,.35) rectangle (11.8,1.8); 
